    <h2 style="margin:52px 0 16px;color:#181818;font-size:34px;line-height:1.12;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-1.15px;">
        Key ingredients to check on the serum label
    </h2>

    <p style="margin:0 0 22px;">
        When evaluating an ingredient list for dehydrated skin, look past upfront marketing claims and check the first ten to fifteen ingredients for these proven performers:
    </p>

    <ul style="margin:0 0 22px;padding-left:22px;">
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>Glycerin and saccharide isomerate.</strong> Glycerin penetrates surface layers to hold water within cells<sup><a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/18510666/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank" style="color:#181818;text-decoration:none;">3</a></sup>. Saccharide isomerate (Pentavitin) binds to skin keratin, delivering hydration that holds through the day without rinsing off easily.</li>
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>Ceramides and cholesterol.</strong> Ceramides make up half of your natural barrier lipids. Pairing ceramides (NP, AP, EOP) with cholesterol restores the mortar between cells, preventing moisture loss and eliminating tight discomfort<sup><a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24656726/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank" style="color:#181818;text-decoration:none;">5</a></sup>.</li>
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>Niacinamide.</strong> Niacinamide stimulates your skin to produce its own ceramides and fatty acids, strengthening the barrier while refining pores and supporting an even tone<sup><a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/17147561/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank" style="color:#181818;text-decoration:none;">6</a></sup>.</li>
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>Centella asiatica (cica).</strong> Cica calms stinging and reactivity, helping irritated skin tolerate daily hydration comfortably<sup><a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24278045/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank" style="color:#181818;text-decoration:none;">7</a></sup>.</li>
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>Peptides and betaine.</strong> Peptides support skin repair and firmness, while betaine stabilizes cellular hydration during shifts in humidity.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 style="margin:52px 0 16px;color:#181818;font-size:34px;line-height:1.12;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-1.15px;">
        Ingredients and formulas to avoid when skin is tight
    </h2>

    <p style="margin:0 0 22px;">
        When your barrier is compromised by dehydration, applying harsh formulas worsens water loss and prolongs tightness. Avoid these common formulation pitfalls:
    </p>

    <ul style="margin:0 0 22px;padding-left:22px;">
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>Drying alcohols.</strong> Denatured or isopropyl alcohol near the top of the ingredient list strips surface lipids and accelerates water loss.</li>
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>Strong fragrance and essential oils.</strong> Citrus, eucalyptus, and heavy fragrance blends trigger stinging on fragile, dehydrated skin.</li>
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>Daily exfoliating acids.</strong> Pause strong glycolic, lactic, or salicylic acid treatments while skin is tight. Exfoliating too often breaks down surface cells faster than your skin can rebuild them.</li>
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>Astringent toners.</strong> Witch hazel and menthol create a brief cooling sensation, but strip moisture and worsen afternoon tightness.</li>
    </ul>

    
    <div style="margin:34px 0;padding:24px 26px;border-top:1px solid #B87330;border-bottom:1px solid #B87330;background:#FFFEF3;">
        <p style="margin:0 0 7px;color:#B87330;font-size:12px;line-height:1.2;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;">Keep in mind</p>
        <p style="margin:0;color:#181818;">
            A hydrating serum supplies water and water-binding factors, but it cannot replace a moisturiser. Always seal your serum with an emollient lotion or cream while your skin is still damp to prevent water from evaporating into the air.
        </p>
    </div>

    <h2 style="margin:52px 0 16px;color:#181818;font-size:34px;line-height:1.12;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-1.15px;">
        How to apply a hydrating serum for best results
    </h2>

    <p style="margin:0 0 22px;">
        How you apply your serum matters as much as the formula itself. Follow these five practical steps morning and evening:
    </p>

    <ol style="margin:0 0 22px;padding-left:24px;">
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>Cleanse gently.</strong> Wash with a mild cleanser and lukewarm water so you do not strip natural lipids.</li>
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>Leave skin damp.</strong> Pat lightly with a towel, leaving surface moisture for humectants to bind and carry inward.</li>
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>Apply three to four drops.</strong> Smooth the serum across your face and neck, pressing gently with your palms rather than rubbing.</li>
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>Seal with moisturiser.</strong> Apply your moisturiser while skin is still dewy to trap the hydration.</li>
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>Finish with sunscreen.</strong> Protect your recovering barrier every morning with broad-spectrum SPF to prevent ultraviolet damage.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2 style="margin:52px 0 16px;color:#181818;font-size:34px;line-height:1.12;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-1.15px;">
        A simple comparison of hydrating serum types
    </h2>

    <p style="margin:0 0 22px;">
        Hydrating serums fall into distinct categories depending on their balance of water-attracting and barrier-sealing ingredients:
    </p>

    <div style="margin:24px 0 34px;overflow-x:auto;border-top:2px solid #181818;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(24,24,24,.25);">
        <table style="width:100%;min-width:620px;border-collapse:collapse;text-align:left;font-size:15px;line-height:1.55;">
